# config/axes/Kconfig.virt — The VIRT axis and the hypervisor core (crates/core/hv)
#
# The role is the VIRT axis (the choice below): none, hosted, split or
# static. The CPU's extension is probed per ISA, each with its n / probe /
# require policy: RV_H (RISC-V H), X86_SVM and X86_VMX (config/arch/Kconfig.isa-*);
# on aarch64 the boot trampoline keeps EL2 (VIRT is not none) when it was
# entered there and the role degrades to `none` (with the reason in the boot
# line) when it was not; with VIRT none it drops to EL1 as before.
# A guest's RAM is MemObj frames that VMMD.ELF creates and charges to its own
# Domain (the kernel pins them and holds a reference until `destroy`), so the
# mem_pages of the VMMD.ELF row must budget its own frames plus the guests'
# RAM (4 KiB pages: mem_mib * 256 each; `vmmd_pages` in the topology builder)
# and the boot refuses the guests when the row is short. The built-in
# topology declares no guest, so its row carries only VMMD.ELF's own frames;
# the signed CAPS.TOML that declares a guest writes the row. A MemObj holds
# MAX_MEMOBJ_PAGES (64) pages and the guests may take half of MAX_MEMOBJS
# (config/limits/Kconfig.limits), so the rows' mem_mib add up to at most
# MAX_MEMOBJS / 2 * 256 KiB on a 4 KiB granule (2 MiB at the default 16
# objects) and the boot refuses rows above it. The qemu-*-hv defconfigs set
# MAX_MEMOBJS to its maximum, 256: 32 MiB of guest RAM at a 4 KiB granule
# (at 876 B of static RAM per slot, 219 KiB for the table). A guest that
# needs more than the table can describe needs a memory object that holds
# more pages, which is not built yet.
#
# Nothing below is asked unless VIRT is not none; the integers are always
# present so the limits crate and the host tests see the same constants.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VIRT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

choice
    prompt "Virtualization role (VIRT)"
    default VIRT_SPLIT if SECURITY_CONFIDENTIAL
    default VIRT_STATIC if PRESET_INDUSTRIAL || PRESET_AUTOMOTIVE
    default VIRT_HOSTED if (PRESET_DESKTOP || PRESET_SERVER) && ARCH_X86_64
    default VIRT_SPLIT if (PRESET_DESKTOP || PRESET_SERVER) && ARCH_AARCH64
    default VIRT_NONE
    help
      The hypervisor role of the image. Anything but `none` builds the
      hypervisor core (config/axes/Kconfig.virt, crates/core/hv): the topology's
      `[guest.NAME]` rows are admitted at boot and each becomes a VM with a
      `vm.<n>` endpoint. The CPU's extension is probed per ISA (RV_H,
      X86_SVM, X86_VMX; EL2 on aarch64) and a CPU without it runs the role as
      `none`, with the reason in the boot line, unless HV_REQUIRE says the
      image is useless without it. What the role may not be combined with is
      a `depends on`. Booting a guest image is a later step.

    config VIRT_NONE
        bool "None"
        help
          The kernel runs on the hardware and hosts no guest.

    config VIRT_HOSTED
        bool "Hosted (guests run beside the kernel)"
        depends on !BOARD_VF2
        help
          The kernel hosts guests with the CPU's virtualization extension
          and keeps its own drivers.

    config VIRT_SPLIT
        bool "Split (a resident monitor under the kernel)"
        depends on !BOARD_VF2
        help
          A small resident monitor owns the virtualization extension and the
          kernel runs as its first guest (the aarch64 EL2 stub shape).

    config VIRT_STATIC
        bool "Static partitioning (pinned guests, fixed at build time)"
        depends on !BOARD_VF2
        help
          Guests are pinned to harts and memory fixed in the signed
          topology; no guest is created at run time.

endchoice

menu "Hypervisor"

config HV
    # cargo-feature: hv
    bool
    default y if !VIRT_NONE
    default n
    help
      The hypervisor core is built in (crates/core/hv): Vm and Vcpu, the
      `vm` and `vcpu` kernel-hosted interfaces, the topology's guest rows
      admitted at boot. Derived from VIRT, never asked.

config HV_REQUIRE
    bool "Refuse to boot when the CPU cannot host the guests"
    depends on HV
    default y if VIRT_STATIC
    default n
    help
      The role says guests run, and the CPU (or firmware, or the way the
      machine was entered) gives no usable virtualization extension. n: the
      boot prints why and goes on without guests. y: the boot halts. A
      static partition is the case where a kernel that boots without its
      guests is a kernel that does not do its job.

config HV_MAX_VCPUS
    int "vCPUs over all guests"
    range 1 64
    default 4 if HWCLASS_EMBEDDED
    default 8
    help
      The size of the vCPU pool (crates/core/hv, `vm::MAX_VCPUS`): the sum of
      `cpus` over the topology's `[guest.NAME]` rows may not exceed it. A
      vCPU is a record (its harts and pending interrupt lines) and, while it
      runs, a kernel thread. The built-in row of VMMD.ELF is granted an
      endpoint for each of the first 16 (the most one monitor serves).

      Cost: 32 B of RAM per vCPU on riscv64 and x86_64; on aarch64 also
      about 3 KiB of static RAM per vCPU (the world-switch block and the
      virtual interrupt controller's queue, crates/core/arch-aarch64
      `ext/hv/vcpu.rs`).

config HV_EXAMPLE_GUEST
    bool "Declare an example guest in the built-in topology"
    depends on HV
    default n
    help
      The built-in topology carries one `[guest.azos]` row: one vCPU,
      HV_EXAMPLE_GUEST_MEM_MIB of RAM, the virtio-console, virtio-blk and
      virtio-net backends and the `cautious` envelope, and the VMMD.ELF row
      budgets the guest's frames (`vmmd_pages`). It is the host half of
      `make azos-guest` (AzOS inside AzOS, tools/run_guest.sh): the guest
      kernel and its disk are files of the volume (`/fat/VM/KERNEL.ELF`,
      the ELF the monitor starts at boot, and `/fat/VM/DISK.IMG`). Only the
      qemu-hv-guest and qemu-aarch64-hv-guest defconfigs set it; the other
      hypervisor defconfigs declare no guest.
      Without the files the guest is declared and never started. A product
      declares its guests in the signed CAPS.TOML (format 7), not here.

      Cost: 40 B plus 16 B per TOPOLOGY_GUEST_DEVICES of RAM, and the guest's
      RAM from the free frames when the guest starts.

config HV_EXAMPLE_GUEST_MEM_MIB
    int "RAM of the example guest (MiB)"
    range 1 32
    default 24
    help
      The example guest's `mem_mib`. An AzOS kernel (qemu-*guest defconfigs)
      boots in 24 MiB. At most 32: a guest's RAM is MemObjs of
      MAX_MEMOBJ_PAGES pages and the guests may take half of MAX_MEMOBJS (the
      qemu-*-hv defconfigs set 256, see above).

      VMMD.ELF maps every MemObj of the RAM into its own address space, and a
      task maps as many as it may own (MAX_MEMOBJS / 2,
      crates/core/sched/src/user_window.rs): 24 MiB is 96 objects at a 4 KiB
      page (24 at aarch64's 16 KiB granule), so the qemu-*-hv defconfigs'
      MAX_MEMOBJS of 256 holds it, and the build refuses a size that would
      not fit (crates/core/ipc/src/memobj.rs). The gate has no row that
      boots the guest yet (tools/ci_check.sh, hv_guest_judge, says why).
      x86_64 has no guest entry (mk/images.mk).

      Cost: none until the guest starts; then this much RAM.

endmenu # Hypervisor
